    "FCFE_Bridge!B11": {
      "formula_pattern": "Text commentary explaining: (1) gap of ₹21,463 cr driven by ₹16,463 cr after-tax interest plus ₹5,000 cr net deleveraging; (2) FCFF discounted at WACC (all investors), FCFE at Ke (equityholders only); (3) if net issuing debt, gap would narrow as more cash flows to equity; (4) dual-build reconciliation serves as mechanical self-audit ensuring no formula errors and consistent assumptions across both methods.",
      "expected_value": "The ₹21,463 cr gap between FCFF and FCFE reflects two components: the ₹16,463 cr after-tax interest burden on equity holders, and the ₹5,000 cr of net debt reduction (deleveraging) which reduces cash available to equity. FCFF is discounted at the firm's WACC to value the entire business, while FCFE is discounted at the cost of equity (Ke) because only equity holders receive the residual cash. If Reliance were net issuing debt rather than deleveraging, the gap would narrow materially—the negative ₹5,000 cr adjustment would become positive, adding back the debt proceeds to equity holders. The dual-method reconciliation (top-down vs. bottom-up) acts as a mechanical self-audit: if FCFF figures don't match to the rupee, it exposes formula errors, sign mistakes on WC, or inconsistent tax rates before valuation even begins.",
      "type": "text",
      "tolerance": 0,
      "points": 25
    },
